#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Refuse any commit message carrying a Co-Authored-By trailer.
#
# Why a hook and not a convention. The rule is written in CLAUDE.md and it
# holds whenever it is in a dispatch brief. It fails the moment one brief
# omits it: on 2026-11-01, 30 of that day's commits carried the trailer and
# every one was a subagent's, because the harness issues a per-session
# reminder asking for it and that reminder reaches every agent. The rule was
# right and unenforced, which is the same shape as a principle with no
# failing test behind it.
#
# Enable per clone with: git config core.hooksPath .githooks
# Written for bash 3.2 (macOS /bin/bash): no mapfile, no ${var^^}.
set -euo pipefail

MSG_FILE=$1

# Comment lines are stripped by git before the message is stored, so a
# trailer inside one is not a trailer. Match only real content lines.
if grep -qiE '^[[:space:]]*Co-Authored-By:' <(grep -v '^#' "$MSG_FILE"); then
  cat >&2 <<'EOF'
commit-msg: commit refused — Co-Authored-By is not used in this repo.

Attribution is a single trailer naming the tool and the model:

    Assisted-by: <tool> (<model>)

for example:

    Assisted-by: Claude Code (Opus 5)
    Assisted-by: Cursor (GPT-5.4)

Both fields are required. The same model driven by a different tool is a
different provenance, so the model alone does not say who produced the
commit.

A second Assisted-by line is allowed only when the commit carries
substantial verbatim content from a different model's output. Reviewer
influence alone never earns a trailer.

Your harness may have told you to add Co-Authored-By. CLAUDE.md overrides
it; see § Commit Policy. Remove the line and commit again.
EOF
  exit 1
fi

exit 0
